      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Controversy persists about optimal body weight. Many experts define \"normal\" (healthy) body mass index (BMI) as 18.5 to 24.9 kg/m(2), 25 to 29.9 kg/m(2) as overweight, and 30 kg/m(2) or greater as obese. Obesity is subdivided into 30 to 34.9 kg/m(2) (Grade 1), 35 to 39.9 kg/m(2) (Grade 2), and 40 kg/m(2) and above (Grade 3). Studies consistently show higher mortality for underweight and Grade 2 or 3 obesity, but results conflict for the overweight category and Grade 1 obesity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To study 30-year risk of death related to baseline BMI."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective cohort study in a multiracial population of 273,843 persons using logistic regression with 7 covariates (sex, age, race-ethnicity, education, marital status, smoking, alcohol intake)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Mortality risk by baseline BMI."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "With average follow-up exceeding 30 years, there were 103,218 deaths: 41,215 attributed to cardiovascular causes and 62,003 to noncardiovascular causes. Odds ratios (and 95% confidence intervals) for all deaths in BMI categories, with a BMI of 18.5 to 24.9 kg/m(2) as the referent, were BMI below 18.5 kg/m(2) = 1.1 (1.0-2.0), BMI 25 to 29 kg/m(2) = 1.1 (1.1-1.2), BMI 30 to 34 kg/m(2) = 1.5 (1.4-1.5), BMI 35 to 39 kg/m(2) = 2.1 (1.9-2.3), and BMI 40 kg/m(2) or higher = 2.7 (2.4-3.0). Disparities existed regarding age, race/ethnicity, cause of death, and interval to death."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Compared with persons with BMI defined as normal, persons who were underweight, overweight, and obese were at increased risk of death over 30 years."
        }
      ]},
